# =============================================================================
# Nkanyezi Tours - .gitattributes
#
# Line endings and file types. Without this file you get two annoying problems:
#   1. Screens of "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings on every commit.
#   2. Diffs that show an entire file as changed when somebody's editor saved
#      it with different line endings - which makes code review impossible.
# =============================================================================

# Let git work out what is text and normalise it to LF in the repository,
# checking it out as CRLF on Windows. Everyone gets the endings their tools
# expect and the repository stays consistent.
* text=auto

# Source and markup - always text
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.aspx      text
*.master    text
*.config    text
*.csproj    text
*.sln       text
*.sql       text
*.css       text
*.js        text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.drawio    text

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary files. Git must never try to merge or diff these - it would corrupt
# them. The Word and PowerPoint documents are the important ones: if two people
# edit the same .docx on different branches, git CANNOT merge it. One version
# has to be chosen and the other person's work is lost.
#
# That is why documents should be written in OneDrive or Google Docs and only
# the finished .docx committed here as a snapshot.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.docx      binary
*.pptx      binary
*.xlsx      binary
*.pdf       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.mdf       binary
*.ldf       binary

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL - the .csproj merge problem.
#
# Old-style Web Forms projects list every file by hand, so ANY two people who
# each add a page will both edit NkanyeziTours.csproj and git will report a
# conflict. The fix is nearly always "keep both lines".
#
# Uncommenting the line below makes git keep both sides automatically. It
# removes most of the pain, but can occasionally leave a duplicated
# <Compile Include> entry, which shows up as a clear MSBuild "duplicate items"
# error that you then delete by hand. Louder failure, but never lost work.
#
# Discuss as a group before turning this on.
#
# *.csproj merge=union
